/**
  @page guides-meshes-connectivity Mesh connectivity
  @brief Prepare and query incidence relations for mesh algorithms.

  @tableofcontents
  @m_footernavigation

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-intro Incidence and adjacency

  Incidence associates an entity with its lower- or higher-dimensional
  neighbors. An edge-to-cell relation answers which cells contain that edge.
  A same-dimensional adjacency relation answers which cells are neighbors
  under the connectivity implementation's convention.

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-notation Relation notation

  The pair @f$(d_1,d_2)@f$ identifies the source and target dimensions.
  `compute(d1, d2)` prepares that relation. Indices inside an incidence row
  identify entities of dimension `d2`.

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-computing Prepare relations

  The two-triangle example explicitly requests the relations used by its
  subsequent edge and cell queries:

  @snippet Geometry/Connectivity.cpp connectivity-mesh

  `Discover` mode creates missing lower-dimensional entities. `Restrict` mode
  computes using existing entities. See @ref guides-connectivity for that
  construction policy.

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-common Select the relation

  @subsection guides-meshes-connectivity-common-2d Two-dimensional meshes

  | Relation | Question |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | `(0, 2)` | Which cells contain a vertex? |
  | `(1, 2)` | Which cells contain an edge? |
  | `(2, 0)` | Which vertices define a cell? |
  | `(2, 1)` | Which edges bound a cell? |
  | `(2, 2)` | Which cells are adjacent? |

  @subsection guides-meshes-connectivity-common-3d Three-dimensional meshes

  Replace cell dimension two by three. Facets then have dimension two;
  `compute(2, 3)` is the face-to-cell relation used by boundary algorithms.
  Edge-based operations can also require `(1, 3)` or `(3, 1)`.

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-boundary Recognize the boundary

  A facet with one incident cell is on the boundary. On a conforming manifold
  mesh, an interior facet has two incident cells. Prepare `(d-1, d)` before
  boundary iteration, boundary integration or essential boundary conditions.

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-access Read an incidence row

  `getIncidence({sourceDimension, targetDimension}, sourceIndex)` returns
  the target indices associated with one source entity.

  @snippet Geometry/Connectivity.cpp connectivity-incidence

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-example Inspect a small mesh

  @image html Geometry/Connectivity.png "Actual edge indices and incident-cell counts from the checked two-triangle example." width=560px

  Build and run `Connectivity`. The full source and exported CSV files are
  explained in @ref examples-geometry-connectivity.

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-performance Reuse prepared relations

  @subsection guides-meshes-connectivity-performance-cost Construction cost

  Relation construction can discover entities and allocate incidence rows.
  Prepare the relations needed by the algorithm before repeated traversal.
  Recompute them after topology changes according to the mesh operation's
  invalidation contract.

  @subsection guides-meshes-connectivity-performance-timing Measure representative work

  A timing study should record mesh counts, requested relation pairs, discovery
  mode and whether the relation was already computed. Use a separate benchmark
  for performance measurements; the tutorial mesh keeps every incidence row
  small enough to inspect.

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-required Algorithm prerequisites

  | Operation | Required relation |
  | :--- | :--- |
  | Boundary traversal and Dirichlet conditions | `(d-1, d)` |
  | Neighbor traversal and balanced partitioning | `(d, d)` |
  | Cell-edge traversal | `(d, 1)` |
  | Vertex-to-cell traversal | `(0, d)` |

  @section guides-meshes-connectivity-see-also Continue

  - @ref guides-meshes-iteration "Mesh iterators"
  - @ref guides-interfaces-dg "One-sided traces on facets"
  - @ref examples-geometry-connectivity "Complete connectivity example"
*/
